# Time and Effort Reporting Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Section 1 — Purpose

This policy establishes requirements for documenting how employee time and effort is distributed across federal awards, cost-share commitments, and other organizational activities. It ensures that compensation charges to federal awards comply with the Uniform Guidance at 2 CFR 200.430 (Compensation — personal services) and that charges reasonably reflect the work actually performed.

## Section 2 — Scope

This policy applies to every employee whose compensation is charged, in whole or in part, to a federal award — including direct charges and any required cost-share or matching commitments. It also applies to Principal Investigators (PIs) and other responsible officials who certify effort, and to the fiscal custodian.

Independent contractors are not subject to effort certification but must document time as required by their agreements and by 2 CFR 200.430(i).

## Section 3 — Definitions

| Term | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| **Effort** | The proportion of time spent on a given activity expressed as a percentage of total compensated activity, which must always total 100%. |
| **Effort certification** | An after-the-fact written confirmation that the distribution of effort reported reasonably reflects the actual work performed during the reporting period. |
| **PI** | The Principal Investigator or project director responsible for the technical and administrative conduct of a federal award. |
| **Fiscal custodian** | The role responsible for maintaining the Organization's financial records, processing payroll allocations, and preparing expenditure reports. |

## Section 4 — Effort Reporting Periods and Deadlines

The Organization uses a semi-annual reporting cycle aligned with its fiscal year (fiscal year end: {{ fiscal_year_end }}):

| Period | Covers | Certification Due |
|--------|--------|-------------------|
| Period 1 | The first six months of the fiscal year | No later than 45 days after Period 1 closes |
| Period 2 | The second six months of the fiscal year, ending {{ fiscal_year_end }} | No later than 45 days after the fiscal year closes |

The fiscal custodian shall distribute effort certification forms to each covered employee and responsible PI no later than 30 days after the close of each period. Certifications must be completed and returned by the deadline shown above.

## Section 5 — Effort Certification Requirements

### 5.1 Who certifies

Each effort certification must be signed by the employee or by a responsible official — typically the PI — who has suitable means of verification that the work was performed. "Suitable means of verification" includes first-hand knowledge of the employee's activities, review of project deliverables, or other evidence that the certifier can attest to the distribution of effort.

### 5.2 What the certification must reflect

The certification must reasonably reflect the actual percentage distribution of effort across all activities for which the employee was compensated during the period, including:

- Each federal award to which compensation was charged;
- Cost-share or matching obligations;
- Other sponsored projects; and
- Non-sponsored organizational activities.

Total effort must equal 100%. Percentages need not be exact but must be a reasonable estimate based on actual work performed.

### 5.3 Form and content

The fiscal custodian shall prepare effort certification forms that include, at a minimum:

- Employee name and position;
- Reporting period;
- Each activity or award with its percentage of total effort;
- A certification statement affirming the distribution reasonably reflects actual work; and
- Signature (physical or electronic) and date.

## Section 6 — Budget Estimates and True-Up

Initial payroll charges to federal awards may be based on budget estimates or other reasonable methods. However, budget estimates alone are not acceptable as the final basis for charges to federal awards (2 CFR 200.430(i)(1)(viii)).

At the end of each reporting period, the fiscal custodian shall compare the effort certified by each employee or PI to the charges recorded in the accounting system. Any variance must be resolved by adjusting the charges so they reflect the effort actually reported. Adjustments must be completed within 60 days after the certification deadline for the applicable period.

Significant variances — defined as a shift of 5 percentage points or more on any single award — must be documented with a brief explanation of the cause and the corrective entry made.

## Section 7 — Internal Controls

The Organization maintains the following controls to verify that compensation charges are allowable, allocable, and reasonable:

1. **Segregation of duties.** The fiscal custodian processes payroll allocations; the PI or employee certifies effort; the Board or Executive Director reviews summary reports.
2. **Reconciliation.** The fiscal custodian reconciles payroll charges against certified effort at least semi-annually per Section 6.
3. **Spot checks.** The Executive Director or Board Treasurer may conduct periodic reviews of effort certifications and supporting documentation.
4. **Award monitoring.** PIs are responsible for monitoring effort on their awards throughout the performance period — not only at certification time — and for promptly notifying the fiscal custodian of material changes in effort distribution.

## Section 8 — Responsibilities

| Role | Responsibilities |
|------|-----------------|
| **Employee** | Track effort across all activities; complete and sign effort certifications by the deadline; notify the PI of material changes in effort distribution promptly. |
| **PI / Responsible Official** | Certify effort for employees under their supervision when the PI has suitable means of verification; monitor award budgets and effort throughout the performance period; approve adjustments. |
| **Fiscal custodian** | Distribute certification forms; process payroll allocations; reconcile charges to certified effort; prepare and post adjustments; maintain records; prepare expenditure reports for sponsors. |
| **Executive Director / Board** | Provide oversight of the effort reporting process; conduct or authorize periodic reviews; ensure policy compliance. |

## Section 9 — Corrections and Adjustments

When an error is identified — whether through the semi-annual true-up, a sponsor audit, an internal review, or self-reporting by an employee — the following steps apply:

1. **Identify.** Document the nature of the error, the affected award(s), the dollar amount, and the reporting period.
2. **Correct.** The fiscal custodian prepares a journal entry to reallocate costs to the correct funding source. Corrections must be processed within 60 days of discovery.
3. **Approve.** The PI (or Executive Director for awards without a PI) reviews and approves the correction in writing.
4. **Notify.** If the correction is material or affects a closed reporting period, the fiscal custodian shall notify the sponsoring agency as required by the award terms.
5. **Document.** Retain the correction, supporting explanation, and approval as part of the award file.

## Section 10 — Record Retention

All effort certifications, supporting documentation, payroll allocation records, reconciliation worksheets, and adjustment records shall be retained for a minimum of three years from the date of submission of the final expenditure report for the applicable federal award, in accordance with 2 CFR 200.334.

If any litigation, claim, audit, or negotiation involving the records is started before the end of the three-year period, the records shall be retained until the matter is fully resolved and all resulting obligations are satisfied.

Records shall be stored and managed in accordance with the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## Section 11 — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually, typically at the Q1 board meeting, to ensure it remains consistent with current federal regulations and sponsor requirements. The date of each review shall be recorded in the board minutes.

